        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.887,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The centre O of the circle that passes through the vertices A, B and C of ΔABC is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "circumcentre",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of ΔABC (Fig. 5.5). The circle is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "circumcircle",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and is said to circumscribe the triangle. Conversely, the triangle is said to be inscribed in the circle. Since, the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of AB and BC is a single point, there is just one such circle for a given triangle. Further, for an acute-angled triangle, the circumcentre lies inside the triangle (see Fig. 5.5). For an obtuse-angled triangle, the circumcentre lies outside the triangle (Fig. 5.6). And for a right-angled triangle, the circumcentre lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse (Fig. 5.7)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
